you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  germany's cabinet agrees to cut carbon  emissions by an  extra 10 percent we speak to climate  activist louisa neubauer who forced the  new proposals  how cities around the world are and  aren't adapting to climate change  and why protecting peatlands could help  save the planet  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions and we aim to take you to the  heart of the climate crisis  explain the data driving the changes  that are already affecting us  but also show you just how far we've  come the german cabinet has approved  plans for ambitious new targets  to reduce carbon emissions it comes  after a landmark ruling from the  country's constitutional court  which found that current laws are  insufficient to protect  future generations one of the  environmental activists involved in the  case is louisa  neubauer she's one of the main  organisers of the  fridays for future school strikes  movement and is hugely influential in  the climate world  i spoke to her earlier and started by  asking if she's satisfied with the new  climate commitments from the german  government  this isn't about what satisfies me or  anyone else but this is about  governments aligning the climate  policies to the science and to paris  and the court in germany ruled that the  government needs to listen to the  science  and you know stick to the paris  agreement because it didn't do so in the  past and now  they did agree in fact on higher targets  but they're still in line with paris  so of course we're going to keep  fighting here well the changes that came  about in germany came about because you  went to court and you  won so is litigation the most effective  way  of making change rather than street  activism no i wouldn't  um i wouldn't put this in some kind of  hierarchy here  what we see is that we need to try  everything we can  and so i would rather understand this as  a huge puzzle and you know litigation is  one  piece of that puzzle and street action  and climate action civil disobedience  and working inside institutions that are  different puzzles and  you know ideally eventually they come up  together and they add up  and they produce that kind of pressure  and awareness that we so desperately  need  i mean i feel you know this this  litigation victory was huge  but we cannot solve the climate crisis  court case by court case  simply because you know these court um  systems worked very very slowly it took  over in a year for the court to decide  that the climate target that was decided  in 2019  isn't ambitious enough so i feel you  know we need to also understand that  these climate  cases litigation cases they can provide  a foundation for our work as  activists to then come back to that and  actually demand  our rights to be turned into politics as  well as the court case you've been  helping organize the school strikes in  germany  how did you first get started in that  what first motivated you to get involved  well you know i i never planned to  become a climate activist  simply because i thought that government  in the 21st century  would get things done and i mean the  science is there  and we see what the climate crisis does  today and i thought you know there were  people who were aware of all that and  would sort things out  you know by the end of the day and when  i found out a couple of years after the  paris agreement was signed  what was happening around the world and  also in germany her governments were  just failing so badly to stick what they  themselves committed  to do i got i got very irritated and  angry  and uh by i don't know by um coincidence  or so  i ended up at the climate conference in  poland in 2018  and i ran into greta tuneback and  apparently she was the only one who  acted like we were in a crisis  on this u.n climate conference and so i  went back home and was like okay i think  i  think  when you do something and i think i need  to do something  let's take a look now some of the day's  other climate news and the first major  offshore wind farm in the united states  has been given the go-ahead  the vineyard wind project will be  located 14 miles off the coast of  massachusetts  it's expected to create enough  electricity to power 400  000 homes in new england and create 3600  jobs  the project's application was cancelled  by donald trump last year  before being reinstated by president  biden after he took office  [Applause]  the eu will tighten regulations on air  pollution  waste and the use of chemicals it's all  part of a plan to protect  public health and address key causes of  biodiversity loss  the eu commission says it wants to cut  premature deaths associated with air  pollution by at least 55 percent  by 2030. and amazon has issued its first  sustainability bond to raise  funds in order to transition to more  environmentally responsible business  practices  the retailer wants to raise one billion  dollars to invest in renewable energy  clean transport greener buildings and  affordable housing  now one in four cities around the world  can't afford to protect themselves  against climate change  even though nearly all are facing  serious risks  the charity carbon disclosure project  received data from 812 cities on their  environmental plans and they found  that 93 of them are facing significant  climate impacts such as flooding  extreme heat and storms but there are  things that cities are doing  to tackle the climate crisis and make  themselves more resilient  so 20 percent have increased tree  planting or created  green spaces 18 have undertaken  flood mapping 15 are improving community  engagement and education  and 14 are developing crisis management  plans including warning and evacuation  systems  but more than half don't yet have a  climate action plan  that means they're more vulnerable to  the effects of climate change  well joining me now is monjour more shed  who's professor in sustainable  engineering at cardiff  university so what does this survey tell  us do you think about the level of  preparedness of the world cities  in the face of climate change  this is um we have known this for a long  time is that  although the science is out there  we know that the impacts of climate  change will be  will be felt but  cities have not prepared themselves  as such what kind of problems could they  face and what kind of steps  might they be able to take to build up  resilience  well as you have mentioned in there that  one of the key impacts the floods for  example  extreme heat a lot of cities  are heavily built up uh they're dense  and  not only because of the climate change  but because of the  uh the the the volume and the density of  buildings  the temperatures will be um you know  there will be  further increase in temperatures which  will have  further impacts uh you also have  uh impacts uh related to age and gender  so elderly and the infirm  and children will be more affected  you also have in some cities you also  have a significant level of uh  population growth in organization which  will also exercise the problem  um the the other uh  the other um at the other end of the  spectrum  you would see that the impacts are not  going to be  felt at the same time throughout the  year  so in some cities we  have seen predictions and projections  that  winters are going to be warmer  in other cities summers are going to be  warmer  so in a way the the impacts will be  differently felt and  is is not a one solution for  all and uh cities particularly in the  south  um have not been able to prepare  themselves  uh for climate change although some of  them are going to be disproportionately  affected by it  professor monjour more shed thanks very  much indeed  the government's introducing a new  scheme which will allow developers to  damage green spaces in one part of  england but then  pay to improve them in another  biodiversity offsetting is part of the  environment bill which was introduced in  the queen's speech  yesterday under the proposed plans  developers will have to  increase the variety of animals and  plants found on each development site  by 10 elsewhere the government hopes it  will allow  more development on green belt land  whilst improving  wildlife habitats gerald tub reports  the escrick park estate in north  yorkshire will be ready  if and when the environment bill is  passed to offset  biodiversity loss from private  development  there's absolutely masses of opportunity  the landowner here was chosen for a  natural  england pilot scheme as a biodiversity  compensation provider  it means that we will be able to  offer uh biodiversity  to what has been destroyed from  development plus an extra cent 10  which is going to be in embodied in the  environmental bill hopefully this year  so at least people who are living in new  housing estates or occupying new offices  will know that somewhere hopefully close  by there's a really flourishing  new wildlife habitat  but this development site near barnsley  shows the concept can cause anger  many local authorities are already  insisting on a biodiversity net gain  guidance on offsetting says to keep as  much within the footprint of the  development as possible  and this has been offset to a location  six miles away which is of no  interest or relevance to the people here  the government insists developers will  be incentivized  to keep wildlife habitats on site  or nearby but it also plans eventually  to allow biodiversity credits to be  bought and sold  on the open market biodiversity  offsetting is here to stay gerard tub  sky news north yorkshire  so the government's using an offsetting  system to improve biodiversity in  england but what other nature-based  solutions are out there  well scientists from the british  ecological society have examined how all  kinds of landscapes could be used  to combat the climate crisis they say we  need to get serious about peatlands  if we want to cut emissions so what's so  great  about pete  [Applause]  if the uk wants to get serious about its  carbon emissions it needs to get serious  about its peatlands  they are the most carbon dense  terrestrial store of carbon on the  planet  they're like giant ever expanding safety  vaults  underneath our feet but and there is a  problem  eighty percent have been damaged there  is  something that just about everyone in  the uk can do if you're a gardener  to stop using peat based composts  so every time you buy a bag you are  helping to destroy  an area of peatland then we can start  looking at how to restore them and that  restoration process  involves re-wetting them we need to then  start looking at the vegetation  that's growing on them and potentially  start to re-vegetate them  these peatlands can be our most  important way  of capturing some of the excess co2  that's in our atmosphere  so it's incredibly important that we  look after our peatlands  coming up on tomorrow's daily climate  show we'll be looking at what british  farmers need to do to help combat  climate change  you can catch that here on sky news at  the same time and on our social channels  and website  thanks for watching see you then  [Music]

